licky SDK: HubSpot Testing Guide
This guide explains how to inject and test the Clicky Web SDK directly into a live SaaS application like HubSpot. We use a browser extension called Tampermonkey to automatically inject our locally built SDK into the live HubSpot DOM, allowing us to test voice, vision, and cursor pointing without modifying HubSpot's actual codebase.

Prerequisites
Build the SDK: Ensure you have built the latest version of the SDK.
bash

cd clicky-web-sdk
npm run build
Start Local Server: Serve the built files locally.
bash

npm run dev
Keep this terminal running. The SDK will be served at http://localhost:5173.
Installation (Tampermonkey Setup)
To make the SDK persist across page loads in HubSpot, we use Tampermonkey.

Install Tampermonkey: Install the Tampermonkey browser extension for Chrome/Edge.
Create a New Script: Click the Tampermonkey extension icon and select Create a new script...
Paste the Injector Code: Delete the default template and paste the following code exactly as it appears:
javascript

// ==UserScript==
// @name         Clicky SDK HubSpot Injector
// @namespace    http://tampermonkey.net/
// @version      2026-06-07
// @description  Automatically injects the local Clicky SDK into HubSpot
// @author       You
// @match        *://*.hubspot.com/*
// @match        *://hubspot.com/*
// @grant        none
// ==/UserScript==
(function() {
    'use strict';
    
    // Fetch the script directly to bypass strict Content Security Policies (CSP)
    fetch('http://localhost:5173/dist/clicky-web-sdk.umd.cjs')
        .then(response => response.text())
        .then(scriptText => {
            // Execute the fetched SDK code
            eval(scriptText);
            
            console.log('✅ Clicky SDK Loaded via Tampermonkey Fetch');
            
            // Initialize the SDK, pointing to the LIVE Cloudflare Worker
            window.ClickySDK.init({
                workerBaseUrl: 'https://round-voice-437d.essora-contactus.workers.dev', 
                appName: 'HubSpot-Dev-Test',
                debug: true,
                privacy: {
                    // Redact sensitive elements before sending DOM to AI
                    privateSelectors: ['.property-value', '[data-selenium-test="deal-amount"]']
                }
            });
            
            console.log('🚀 Clicky SDK Initialized on HubSpot automatically.');
        })
        .catch(err => console.error("Tampermonkey failed to load local Clicky SDK:", err));
})();
Save: Press Ctrl+S (or Cmd+S) to save the script.
Testing Checklist
Once the script is saved and your local npm run dev server is running, navigate to HubSpot.

1. Verify Initialization
Log into HubSpot.
You should see the Clicky panel/microphone button appear on the screen.
Open DevTools (F12) -> Console. You should see:
✅ Clicky SDK Loaded via Tampermonkey Fetch
🚀 Clicky SDK Initialized on HubSpot automatically.
(Note: You can safely ignore any red CSP or raven.js errors in the console; these are normal HubSpot errors).
2. Test Context & Vision (Does it see the page?)
Navigate to a Contact or Deal record. Click the mic and ask:

"Can you tell me what page I'm on right now?" "What are the names of the top three contacts you see on my screen?"

Expected Result: The AI should respond verbally (via ElevenLabs) reading the specific data it sees in the live DOM.

3. Test the Cursor Overlay (Can it point?)
Ensure a specific button is visible on your screen (e.g., the "Create contact" or "Add" button). Click the mic and ask:

"Can you point to the 'Add' button?"

Expected Result: The blue cursor overlay should appear, animate across the screen, and land directly on the button. (Note: If the button is scrolled off-screen, the console will show target outside viewport).

4. Test Navigation Persistence
Click a link to navigate to a different module in HubSpot (e.g., from Contacts to Marketing). Expected Result: The Clicky UI should briefly disappear and automatically reappear on the new page, ready for new questions.